                'Predicting mathematically, before sunset, whether the crescent will be visible has long been one of the central problems of astronomy. This lesson reviews the historical development of visibility criteria from antiquity to today\'s empirical criteria.',
                'First Period: The Babylonians (about 2,500 years ago)',
                'The first written and systematic attempts to predict crescent visibility go back to Babylonian civilization in Mesopotamia. Babylonian astronomers recorded the state of the crescent on clay tablets. According to research in the history of astronomy, their rules were based on statistical observation and used parameters such as the age of the crescent and the time interval between sunset and moonset (lag time). For example, research mentions a lag-time threshold of about 48 minutes. These rules were empirical and were not based on a geometric model of the Moon\'s phases.',
