              num: '4.5',
              title: 'Limits of Empirical Criteria Versus Theoretical Models (Schaefer\'s Model)',
              body: [
                'Criteria such as Yallop\'s and Odeh\'s are empirical, not fundamental physical models. Understanding this difference is necessary for appreciating the limits of computational predictions in field observation.',
                'Limits of Empirical Criteria',
                'Empirical criteria are built from historical observational data: reports of past observations are plotted on a graph and a curve is fitted to them. The implicit assumption of these criteria is that if an observer in the past saw a crescent of a given width and altitude, another observer under similar conditions could also see it. But these criteria do not take into account the actual state of the atmosphere at the time and place of observation, and they rest on a past statistical sample. For example, the Yallop formula is unaware of a temperature inversion, high humidity or local dust on the western horizon of the observing site.',
                'Schaefer\'s Theoretical Model',
                'Bradley E. Schaefer, an astrophysicist, presented in the 1980s and 1990s models based on the physics of light and the physiology of vision that simulate the path of light from the Moon to the observer\'s eye. The four principal components of such models are:',
                '1. Lunar photometry: the amount of light reflected from the crescent\'s limb.',
                '2. Atmospheric extinction: the absorption and scattering of light in the atmosphere by suspended particles (aerosols), water vapor and ozone.',
                '3. Sky brightness: the brightness of the sky background in twilight.',
                '4. Eye physiology: whether the contrast between the attenuated light of the crescent and the background sky exceeds the human eye\'s detection threshold.',
                'Difficulty of General Use',
                'Applying such models requires accurate environmental information at the time of observation: the aerosol optical depth (AOD), relative humidity, temperature and even the observer\'s visual acuity. Without these data, which are usually unavailable, the model\'s output will not be reliable. For this reason empirical criteria are more stable and practical for use in software, even though their physical basis is simpler.',
              ],
