                'The evening crescent lies near the Sun, which is setting or has just set. This position makes crescent sighting one of the most hazardous branches of observational astronomy. Viewing the Sun through an optical instrument without a suitable filter can cause permanent, irreparable eye damage.',
                'The Hazard of Optical Instruments',
                'Telescopes and binoculars are light collectors. Binoculars with a 50 mm aperture, because their aperture area is far larger than the pupil of the eye (about 7 mm in darkness), gather about 50 times more light than the naked eye and concentrate it on a small spot of the retina. A brief look at the Sun through an unfiltered instrument can burn the retina (especially the macula) and permanently destroy part of central vision. Such damage is painless and may not be felt at the moment it occurs.',
