                'Retinal Biology: Cones and Rods',
                'The retina has two types of photoreceptor:',
                '1. Cones: concentrated in the central region of the retina (the fovea, or yellow spot). They are responsible for color and fine-detail vision, but require relatively bright light to function.',
                '2. Rods: scattered across the peripheral part of the retina and absent from the fovea. They do not distinguish color and have lower resolving power, but are highly sensitive to weak light.',
                'The Problem With Looking Directly at a Faint Target',
