                'Solar filters (Lesson 5.1) are used only to protect the eye from direct sunlight and are not needed once the Sun has fully set. Another group of filters, "contrast-enhancing filters," usually screw into the eyepiece and are used to reduce the unwanted light of the sky background. The effect of these filters differs with atmospheric conditions and the position of the crescent.',
                'Types of Filters and Their Mechanisms',
                'After sunset the sky is still bright with sunlight scattered in the atmosphere. The purpose of the filters is to manage this scattered light.',
                '1. Colored filters (red, orange, yellow): twilight sky light arises mainly from Rayleigh scattering, which is stronger at shorter wavelengths (blue). A red or orange colored filter (such as Wratten #25) reduces the blue part of the light and in many conditions darkens the sky background. The crescent\'s light, which has a broader spectrum, passes through the filter to some degree. The result may be an increase in the crescent\'s contrast against the background. The effect of the filter depends on atmospheric conditions and the crescent\'s position.',
