                'For s Khoei , Golpayegani  and Vahid Khorasani, the compiled source gives only the common wording of the ruling on ways of establishing the month ("that a person sees the Moon himself"), and they are classified under this foundation.',
                'Arguments for This Foundation (as Formulated by  Sistani)',
                '1. The Crescent Is a Universal Timekeeper for People',
                'The Quran describes the new moons as "measurements of time for the people and for Hajj" (2:189). The crescent is a natural calendar for all people, whatever their level of literacy and wherever they live, and the order of social life is impossible without a precise and common calendar. A distinguishing feature of Islamic rulings is that their measures are natural and accessible to all (such as the span of a hand, a stride, sunset, dawn and the sun\'s meridian passage). What qualifies as a universal timekeeper is a crescent visible to the unaided eye; a crescent visible only through binoculars or a telescope, which disappears once the instrument is removed, does not qualify to be a universal timekeeper.',
                '2. The Practice of the Prophet (PBUH) and the Imams (AS)',
                'If the Sharia criterion were sighting with a telescope, it would follow that fasting, Eid al-Fitr, Hajj and other acts tied to specific days of the year would, in some cases, not have fallen on their true days. Yet these noble figures relied on unaided-eye sighting for the start of the lunar month, and the narrations are directed away from sighting with magnifying instruments.',
                '3. The Customary Calendar and the "Temporal Threshold" of the Month',
                'In the conventional calendar in use since antiquity, the first night of the month was the night on which the crescent, after two or three nights of invisibility, reached a condition and position at which it could be seen with the unaided eye. This threshold is a "temporal limit" for the start of the month, just as the limit of exemption (hadd al-tarakhkhus) is a spatial limit for a traveler to shorten prayer. If the temporal limit were taken to be visibility through instruments, in many cases it would fall one night earlier than the time of unaided-eye visibility. The criterion for the start of the month would then differ from the customary calendar of the era of the Prophet (PBUH) and the Imams (AS), and one would have to accept that Islam introduced a new calendar that the general public of that time could not access; this is unacceptable.',
