                'With the invention of new instruments and the recording of new crescent-sighting records, the visibility limit keeps moving back. If sighting with the aided eye were valid, it would follow that the starts of many past months were set later than the reality for lack of stronger instruments, and that future instruments may reveal that some months began earlier than is now believed. This is incompatible with the stability and universality of a Sharia criterion.',
                '5. Preventing Crescent Searching From Becoming Pointless in the Narrations',
                'The purpose of ordinary crescent searching in the era of the Infallibles (AS), done by eye without instruments, was that if the crescent was seen the new month had begun, and if the sky was clear and it was not seen, the month began the following night. If the criterion were visibility through powerful telescopes, failure to see it with the eye, even in a clear sky, would prove nothing, and both the searching and the narrations\' emphasis on it would be pointless. For example, in a narration from Imam Kazim (AS) on fasting on the Day of Doubt, it is stated that when the sky is clear and the crescent has not been seen, no doubt remains that would lead one to fast out of the possibility that the day is part of Ramadan.',
