                'The MABIMS criterion (2021 version): a calendrical and official criterion of the countries of Southeast Asia. A green category for MABIMS means the official conditions of these countries for the start of the month are met (Lesson 4.4).',
                'The Soroudi criterion: a combined criterion in the app that classifies on the basis of a consensus of several geometric criteria (Lesson 4.4).',
                '2. The Color-Code Guide',
                'A guide appears at the bottom of the page. It applies in standard form to most criteria (especially Yallop):',
                'A (dark green): easily visible.',
                'B (light green): sighting is possible under suitable atmospheric conditions.',
                'C (yellow): an optical instrument may be needed to find the crescent.',
                'D (orange): an optical instrument is required.',
                'E (red): not visible even with a telescope.',
                'F (gray/black): not visible by that criterion (the Danjon-limit status is shown separately).',
                '3. Field Strategy: Why Is Simultaneous Comparison Important?',
                'Comparing criteria is useful for identifying critical crescents:',
                'Confidence case: if all criteria (from Yallop to Odeh and MABIMS) show category A, the crescent is easy and heavy equipment is not needed.',
                'Alert case: if the crescent is on the border of visibility and the criteria give different results (for example Yallop C, Odeh B and SAAO D), the observer understands that the crescent is very critical. In that case suitable instruments, a site with clean air and an open horizon, and all field techniques should be used. A sighting of such a crescent, particularly with the naked eye, if properly recorded and documented, will be valuable data for evaluating the criteria. The report must be recorded carefully and with detail (Lessons 11.1 and 12.1).',
                'By displaying the criteria simultaneously, instead of one definitive answer, the app shows a range of scientific possibilities so that the observer can enter the observing site adequately prepared.',
